.TH "vsearch-fastx_uniques" "1" "September 18, 2026" "version 2.32.0" "vsearch manual"
.hy
.SH NAME
.PP
vsearch --fastx_uniques \[em] merge strictly identical fasta or fastq
sequences
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
\f[B]vsearch\f[R] \f[B]--fastx_uniques\f[R] \f[I]fastxfile\f[R]
(\f[B]--fastaout\f[R] | \f[B]--fastqout\f[R]) \f[I]filename\f[R]
[\f[I]options\f[R]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The vsearch command \f[V]--fastx_uniques\f[R] groups strictly identical
sequences from a fasta or fastq file, like \f[V]--derep_fulllength\f[R]
(see \f[V]vsearch-derep_fulllength(1)\f[R]).
The unique sequences are written to \f[V]--fastaout\f[R] and/or
\f[V]--fastqout\f[R], sorted by decreasing abundance (ties are broken by
header, in alphanumerical order, then by input order).
\f[V]--fastqout\f[R] requires fastq input; \f[V]--maxuniquesize\f[R],
\f[V]--minuniquesize\f[R] and \f[V]--topn\f[R] filter the fasta and
fastq outputs only, while the \f[V]--uc\f[R] and \f[V]--tabbedout\f[R]
outputs always list all clusters.
.PP
When the input is a fastq file, quality scores in the output correspond
by default to the average error probability at each position across all
grouped sequences (see \f[V]vsearch-expected_error(7)\f[R]).
Use \f[V]--fastq_qout_max\f[R] to use the best (highest) quality score
observed at each position instead.
Output quality scores are encoded with the \f[V]--fastq_asciiout\f[R]
offset and clamped to the range defined by \f[V]--fastq_qminout\f[R] and
\f[V]--fastq_qmaxout\f[R]; a quality score of 0 is written as 1, since
both stand for the same uninformative error probability (0.75).
.PP
The average is accumulated one grouped sequence at a time, and the
running value is converted back to a quality score at every step.
A score is therefore a poor estimator of the group\[cq]s mean quality
once the group is large: because a quality score stands for the highest
error probability it can represent, a grouped sequence that disagrees
downward at a position lowers the group\[cq]s score there, while one
that agrees upward leaves it unchanged as soon as the group carries much
more abundance than the incoming sequence.
A high-abundance group\[cq]s scores thus reflect how many of its members
disagreed downward at each position rather than the average of their
scores.
\f[V]--fastq_qout_max\f[R] is not affected, being independent of order
and abundance.
.PP
This command is not multithreaded.
The inverse operation is \f[V]--rereplicate\f[R] (see
\f[V]vsearch-rereplicate(1)\f[R]).
.PP
See \f[V]vsearch-fasta(5)\f[R] and \f[V]vsearch-fastq(5)\f[R] for a
description of the input formats.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS mandatory options
.PP
At least one of \f[V]--fastaout\f[R], \f[V]--fastqout\f[R],
\f[V]--tabbedout\f[R], or \f[V]--uc\f[R] must be specified.
.SS core options
.TP
\f[V]--fastaout\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write the dereplicated sequences to \f[I]filename\f[R], in fasta format,
sorted by decreasing abundance.
Each unique sequence retains the header of the first occurrence in the
input.
.TP
\f[V]--fastqout\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write the dereplicated sequences to \f[I]filename\f[R], in fastq format
(see \f[V]vsearch-fastq(5)\f[R]), sorted by decreasing abundance.
Quality scores correspond to the average error probability at each
position across all grouped sequences, or to the best quality score if
\f[V]--fastq_qout_max\f[R] is set.
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_qout_max\f[R]
Use the best (highest) quality score observed at each position when
computing quality scores for fastq output, instead of averaging error
probabilities.
.TP
\f[V]--maxuniquesize\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Discard sequences with a post-dereplication abundance greater than
\f[I]positive integer\f[R].
When that value is smaller than the \f[V]--minuniquesize\f[R] value, no
sequence can satisfy the abundance filter: vsearch issues a warning and
writes no sequence.
.RS
.PP
The filter applies to the sequence output only.
A cluster summary written with \f[V]--uc\f[R], where the command offers
it, still lists every cluster, discarded ones included.
This matches usearch.
.RE
.TP
\f[V]--minuniquesize\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Discard sequences with a post-dereplication abundance smaller than
\f[I]positive integer\f[R].
When that value is larger than the \f[V]--maxuniquesize\f[R] value, no
sequence can satisfy the abundance filter: vsearch issues a warning and
writes no sequence.
.RS
.PP
The filter applies to the sequence output only.
A cluster summary written with \f[V]--uc\f[R], where the command offers
it, still lists every cluster, discarded ones included.
This matches usearch.
.RE
.TP
\f[V]--sizein\f[R]
Use the abundance annotations present in sequence headers when reading
fasta or fastq file.
Search for the pattern \f[V][>\[at];]size=integer[;]\f[R].
Entries without abundance annotations are silently assumed to be of
\f[V]size=1\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--sizeout\f[R]
Add abundance annotations to sequence headers when writing fasta or
fastq files.
Add the pattern \f[V];size=integer\f[R], where the integer is the total
abundance of the cluster: the sum of the members\[cq] abundance
annotations when \f[V]--sizein\f[R] is used, or the number of merged
sequences otherwise.
.TP
\f[V]--strand\f[R] \f[I]plus\f[R]|\f[I]both\f[R]
Check the \f[I]plus\f[R] strand only (default), or check \f[I]both\f[R]
strands when comparing sequences.
Keywords are case-insensitive.
.TP
\f[V]--tabbedout\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write dereplication details to \f[I]filename\f[R] as a tab-separated
file with one row per input sequence and 6 columns:
.RS
.IP "1." 3
original sequence label;
.IP "2." 3
output label (label of the first sequence in the cluster, possibly
relabelled);
.IP "3." 3
cluster number (zero-based);
.IP "4." 3
sequence number within the cluster (zero-based);
.IP "5." 3
cluster size (the number of sequences merged into the cluster, whether
or not \f[V]--sizein\f[R] is used);
.IP "6." 3
original label of the first sequence in the cluster (before any
relabelling).
.RE
.TP
\f[V]--topn\f[R] \f[I]positive non-null integer\f[R]
Write only the \f[I]n\f[R] first entries.
The parameter \f[I]n\f[R] must be greater than zero, and is silently
ignored if it is greater than the total number of entries.
.TP
\f[V]--uc\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write results to \f[I]filename\f[R] in a tab-separated uclust-like
format with 10 columns.
Three record types are used per row: cluster seeds (\f[V]S\f[R]), hits
(\f[V]H\f[R]), and cluster summaries (\f[V]C\f[R]).
Columns are:
.RS
.IP " 1." 4
record type (\f[V]S\f[R], \f[V]H\f[R], or \f[V]C\f[R]);
.IP " 2." 4
cluster number (zero-based);
.IP " 3." 4
centroid length (\f[V]S\f[R]), query length (\f[V]H\f[R]), or cluster
size (\f[V]C\f[R]): the number of sequences in the cluster, or their
total abundance when \f[V]--sizein\f[R] is used;
.IP " 4." 4
percent identity with centroid (\f[V]H\f[R]), or \f[V]*\f[R]
(\f[V]S\f[R], \f[V]C\f[R]);
.IP " 5." 4
match orientation \f[V]+\f[R] or \f[V]-\f[R] (\f[V]H\f[R]), or
\f[V]*\f[R] (\f[V]S\f[R], \f[V]C\f[R]);
.IP " 6." 4
not used; always \f[V]0\f[R] (\f[V]H\f[R]) or \f[V]*\f[R] (\f[V]S\f[R],
\f[V]C\f[R]);
.IP " 7." 4
not used; always \f[V]0\f[R] (\f[V]H\f[R]) or \f[V]*\f[R] (\f[V]S\f[R],
\f[V]C\f[R]);
.IP " 8." 4
CIGAR alignment string (\f[V]H\f[R]), or \f[V]*\f[R] (\f[V]S\f[R],
\f[V]C\f[R]); \f[V]=\f[R] denotes strictly identical sequences (with
\f[V]--cluster_fast\f[R], identical ignoring terminal gaps); the
dereplication commands always write \f[V]*\f[R] here; see
\f[V]vsearch-cigar(5)\f[R];
.IP " 9." 4
query label (\f[V]H\f[R]), or centroid label (\f[V]S\f[R], \f[V]C\f[R]);
.IP "10." 4
centroid label (\f[V]H\f[R]), or \f[V]*\f[R] (\f[V]S\f[R], \f[V]C\f[R]).
.RE
.SS secondary options
.TP
\f[V]--bzip2_decompress\f[R]
Specify that the \f[I]input pipe\f[R] is streaming data compressed using
Huffman coding.
See \f[V]bzip2(1)\f[R] for more details.
This option is required when compressed data arrives on standard input
through a pipe (`-'), where the format cannot be detected without
consuming the stream.
It is not needed when reading from a regular file compressed with bzip2,
nor when such a file is redirected to standard input: compression is
then detected automatically, and a contradicting option is ignored (with
a warning when the input is standard input).
Pipes other than standard input, such as shell process substitutions and
named FIFOs, are always read as uncompressed data; compressed data must
arrive on standard input or as a named file.
.TP
\f[V]--fasta_width\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Set the maximal width of sequences when writing fasta files.
Longer sequences are folded and written on several lines.
Default width is 80 nucleotides.
Set to zero (0) to suppress folding.
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_ascii\f[R] 33|64
Specify the \f[I]offset\f[R] used as the basis for the fastq quality
score when reading fastq files.
For example, an offset of 33 means that a quality value of 41 is
represented by the 74th ASCII symbol (33 + 41 = 74), which is `J'.
See \f[V]ascii(7)\f[R] for a view of the ASCII character set.
The offset value is either 33 or 64, default is 33.
.PP
The offset matters even to a command that never decodes a quality score:
it is what the reader compares the observed quality symbols against
before warning that the file may use the other encoding, it sets the
default of \f[V]--fastq_qmax\f[R] (the highest score the offset can
represent), and the sum rules on \f[V]--fastq_qmin\f[R] and
\f[V]--fastq_qmax\f[R] are stated in terms of it.
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_asciiout\f[R] 33|64
Specify the \f[I]offset\f[R] used as the basis for the fastq quality
score when writing fastq output files.
For example, an offset of 33 means that a quality value of 41 is
represented by the 74th ASCII symbol (33 + 41 = 74), which is `J'.
See \f[V]ascii(7)\f[R] for a view of the ASCII character set.
The offset value is either 33 or 64, default is 33.
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_qmax\f[R] \f[I]integer\f[R]
Specify the maximal quality score accepted when reading fastq sequences.
Stop with an error message if a quality score higher than the specified
value is read.
The offset (see \f[V]--fastq_ascii\f[R]) plus the maximal score may not
exceed 126, the last printable ASCII character: scores up to 93 with
offset 33, up to 62 with offset 64.
The default is the highest score the offset can represent (93 with
offset 33, 62 with offset 64), so no quality score is rejected unless
this option is lowered.
Before version 2.32.0 the default was 41, the usual maximum for
Sanger/Illumina 1.8+ files, which rejected PacBio HiFi and nanopore
files outright.
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_qmaxout\f[R] \f[I]integer\f[R]
Specify the maximum quality score used when writing fastq files.
The default is the highest score the output offset can represent (93
with \f[V]--fastq_asciiout\f[R] 33, 62 with offset 64), so scores read
from the input are written back unchanged.
Before version 2.32.0 the default was 41, the usual maximum for
Sanger/Illumina 1.8+ files, which silently reduced any higher score.
Older formats may use a maximum quality score of 40.
Two commands are exceptions and keep the old default of 41, because they
\f[I]generate\f[R] the score they clamp instead of passing one through:
\f[V]--fasta2fastq\f[R], which has no input quality and uses this option
as the value to write, and \f[V]--fastq_mergepairs\f[R], which caps the
computed posterior quality of a merged base.
.PP
For \f[V]--fastq_mergepairs\f[R] the offset in question is
\f[V]--fastq_ascii\f[R], not \f[V]--fastq_asciiout\f[R]: that command
writes fastq but does not accept \f[V]--fastq_asciiout\f[R], so a merged
quality symbol carries the same offset the input was read with.
The sum rule is stated against \f[V]--fastq_ascii\f[R] there, and
against \f[V]--fastq_asciiout\f[R] everywhere else.
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_qmin\f[R] \f[I]integer\f[R]
Specify the minimal quality score accepted when reading fastq sequences.
Stop with an error message if a quality score lower than the specified
value is read.
The offset (see \f[V]--fastq_ascii\f[R]) plus the minimal score must be
at least 33, the first printable ASCII character: scores down to 0 with
offset 33, down to -31 with offset 64.
The value may therefore be negative, which is what the negative scores
of older formats require, but note that those formats are not supported
(see \f[V]vsearch-fastq(5)\f[R]).
The default is 0, which is usual for recent Sanger/Illumina 1.8+ files.
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_qminout\f[R] \f[I]integer\f[R]
Specify the minimum quality score used when writing fastq files.
The offset (see \f[V]--fastq_asciiout\f[R]) plus the minimum score must
be at least 33, so the value may be negative when the output offset is
64.
The default is 0, which is usual for recent Sanger/Illumina 1.8+ files.
Older formats may use scores between -5 and 2.
.PP
For \f[V]--fastq_mergepairs\f[R] the offset in question is
\f[V]--fastq_ascii\f[R], not \f[V]--fastq_asciiout\f[R]: that command
writes fastq but does not accept \f[V]--fastq_asciiout\f[R], so a merged
quality symbol carries the same offset the input was read with, and the
bound follows it.
.PP
A negative minimum matters where the score passes through vsearch and
may itself be negative: \f[V]--fastq_convert\f[R] on an offset-64 file
read with a lowered \f[V]--fastq_qmin\f[R] writes the score back
unchanged with \f[V]--fastq_qminout\f[R] -5, where the default 0 would
raise it.
It has no effect where vsearch \f[I]computes\f[R] the score it clamps,
because such a score is derived from an error probability and is
therefore never negative: \f[V]--fastq_mergepairs\f[R], and
\f[V]--fastq_convert --fastq_solexa\f[R], which converts to the Phred
scale before any output clamp applies.
.TP
\f[V]--gzip_decompress\f[R]
Specify that the \f[I]input pipe\f[R] is streaming data compressed using
Lempel-Ziv coding.
See \f[V]gzip(1)\f[R] for more details.
This option is required when compressed data arrives on standard input
through a pipe (`-'), where the format cannot be detected without
consuming the stream.
It is not needed when reading from a regular file compressed with gzip,
nor when such a file is redirected to standard input: compression is
then detected automatically, and a contradicting option is ignored (with
a warning when the input is standard input).
Pipes other than standard input, such as shell process substitutions and
named FIFOs, are always read as uncompressed data; compressed data must
arrive on standard input or as a named file.
.TP
\f[V]--label_suffix\f[R] \f[I]string\f[R]
Add the suffix \f[I]string\f[R] to sequence headers when writing fasta
or fastq files.
For example, with \f[V]--label_suffix \[dq];status=healthy\[dq]\f[R],
sequence header `>seq1' becomes `>seq1;status=healthy'.
.TP
\f[V]--lengthout\f[R]
Add a sequence length annotation (\f[V];length=integer\f[R]) to each
sequence header when writing fasta or fastq files.
.TP
\f[V]--log\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write messages to \f[I]filename\f[R].
Messages include program version, start and finish times, elapsed time,
amount of memory available, maximum amount of memory consumed, number of
cores and command line options, and if need be, command-specific
informational messages, warnings, and errors.
Messages are written \f[I]in addition\f[R] to the standard error, not
instead of it; use \f[V]--quiet\f[R] to silence the standard error copy
(see the \f[V]--quiet\f[R] entry for what it spares).
.TP
\f[V]--maxseqlength\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Discard sequences longer than \f[I]positive integer\f[R] (50,000
nucleotides by default).
The value must not exceed 2,147,481,646 (\f[V]INT_MAX\f[R] minus 2,001).
When that value is smaller than the effective \f[V]--minseqlength\f[R]
value (whose default is command-specific), no sequence can pass the
length filter: vsearch issues a warning.
.TP
\f[V]--minseqlength\f[R] \f[I]non-negative integer\f[R]
Discard sequences shorter than \f[I]non-negative integer\f[R] (1
nucleotide by default).
A value of 0 retains empty sequences.
When that value is larger than the \f[V]--maxseqlength\f[R] value, no
sequence can pass the length filter: vsearch issues a warning.
.TP
\f[V]--no_progress\f[R]
Suppress the gradually increasing progress indicator normally written to
the \f[I]standard error\f[R] \f[V]stderr(3)\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--notrunclabels\f[R]
Retain whole sequence headers in output files.
By default, vsearch truncates sequence headers at first space or
tabulation.
This option suppresses truncation.
.TP
\f[V]--quiet\f[R]
Suppress messages to the \f[I]standard output\f[R] \f[V]stdout(3)\f[R]
and \f[I]standard error\f[R] \f[V]stderr(3)\f[R], except for warnings
and error messages.
.TP
\f[V]--relabel\f[R] \f[I]string\f[R]
Replace sequence headers with the prefix \f[I]string\f[R] and a ticker
(1, 2, 3, etc.).
For example, with \f[V]--relabel \[dq]cluster:\[dq]\f[R], the first
sequence header becomes `>cluster:1', the second sequence header becomes
`>cluster:2', and so on.
To retain annotations, use their corresponding options
(\f[V]--lengthout\f[R], \f[V]--eeout\f[R], and \f[V]--sizeout\f[R]).
Use \f[V]--relabel_keep\f[R] to also retain old sequence identifiers.
.TP
\f[V]--relabel_keep\f[R]
Retain old sequence identifiers by including them at the end of the new
headers, after a space.
.TP
\f[V]--relabel_md5\f[R]
Replace each sequence header with the MD5 digest derived from the
sequence itself.
The sequence is converted to upper case, and each `U' is replaced with a
`T' before computation of the digest.
The MD5 digest is a 128-bit value (16 bytes), represented using a string
of 32 ASCII characters.
Each pair of characters encodes an hexadecimal value, ranging from
\f[V]x00\f[R] to \f[V]xff\f[R].
See \f[V]md5(3)\f[R] for more details, and \f[V]--relabel_sha1\f[R] for
an alternative hashing algorithm.
To retain annotations, use their corresponding options
(\f[V]--lengthout\f[R], \f[V]--eeout\f[R], and \f[V]--sizeout\f[R]).
Use \f[V]--relabel_keep\f[R] to also retain old sequence identifiers.
.TP
\f[V]--relabel_self\f[R]
Replace each sequence header with the sequence itself.
To retain annotations, use their corresponding options
(\f[V]--lengthout\f[R], \f[V]--eeout\f[R], and \f[V]--sizeout\f[R]).
Use \f[V]--relabel_keep\f[R] to also retain old sequence identifiers.
.TP
\f[V]--relabel_sha1\f[R]
Replace each sequence header with the SHA1 digest derived from the
sequence itself.
The sequence is converted to upper case, and each `U' is replaced with a
`T' before computation of the digest.
The SHA1 digest is a 160-bit value (20 bytes), represented using a
string of 40 ASCII characters.
Each pair of characters encodes an hexadecimal value, ranging from
\f[V]x00\f[R] to \f[V]xff\f[R].
See \f[V]sha1(3)\f[R] for more details, and \f[V]--relabel_md5\f[R] for
an alternative hashing algorithm.
To retain annotations, use their corresponding options
(\f[V]--lengthout\f[R], \f[V]--eeout\f[R], and \f[V]--sizeout\f[R]).
Use \f[V]--relabel_keep\f[R] to also retain old sequence identifiers.
.TP
\f[V]--sample\f[R] \f[I]string\f[R]
Add the given sample identifier \f[I]string\f[R] to sequence headers
when writing fasta or fastq files.
For instance, if \f[I]string\f[R] is `ABC', the text
\f[V];sample=ABC\f[R] will be added to the headers.
\f[I]string\f[R] is silently truncated at the first `;' or whitespace
character (space, tab, newline, carriage return, vertical tab or form
feed), so such characters should not be used in \f[I]string\f[R].
Other characters (alphabetical, numerical and punctuations) are
accepted.
When nothing is left after truncation \[em] an empty \f[I]string\f[R],
or one starting with `;' or a blank character \[em] vsearch issues a
warning and writes a bare \f[V];sample=\f[R] annotation.
.TP
\f[V]--xee\f[R]
Strip expected error (ee) annotations from sequence headers when writing
fasta or fastq files.
Search for the pattern \f[V][>\[at];]ee=float[;]\f[R].
Expected error annotations are added by the synonymous options
\f[V]--fastq_eeout\f[R] and \f[V]--eeout\f[R] described in
\f[V]vsearch-fastx_filter(1)\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--xlength\f[R]
Strip sequence length annotations from sequence headers when writing
fasta or fastq files.
Search for the pattern \f[V][>\[at];]length=integer[;]\f[R].
Sequence length annotations are added by the \f[V]--lengthout\f[R]
option.
.TP
\f[V]--xsize\f[R]
Strip abundance annotations from sequence headers when writing fasta or
fastq files.
Search for the pattern \f[V][>\[at];]size=integer[;]\f[R].
Abundance annotations are added by the \f[V]--sizeout\f[R] option.
.SS ignored options
.TP
\f[V]--threads\f[R] \f[I]positive non-null integer\f[R]
Command is not multithreaded, option has no effect (a warning is printed
when more than one thread is requested).
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Dereplicate sequences in \f[I]input.fasta\f[R], annotate with abundance,
write to \f[I]derep.fasta\f[R], and record clustering details in
\f[I]derep.uc\f[R]:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
vsearch \[rs]
    --fastx_uniques input.fasta \[rs]
    --sizeout \[rs]
    --fastaout derep.fasta \[rs]
    --uc derep.uc
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Dereplicate a fastq file, keeping only the best quality score at each
position:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
vsearch \[rs]
    --fastx_uniques input.fastq \[rs]
    --fastq_qout_max \[rs]
    --sizeout \[rs]
    --fastqout derep.fastq
\f[R]
.fi
